  "input": {
    "rationale": "Consistency fix in the knowledge record. The statement about which entry points the proposal gate covers now exists twice: the original entry recorded it as source-derived and explicitly not executed, and a second entry created in the previous batch carries the same statement with the read-index receipt. The original entry is corrected to point at that executed evidence, so no reviewer reads a stale claim that the boundary was never observed. No interpretation used the original entry, so no use needs re-evaluating.",
    "sources": [],
    "investigations": [],
    "knowledge": [
      {
        "id": "7242db61fc07405aa9322f9af5811568-knowledge-0",
        "ref": null,
        "kind": "behavior",
        "statement": "The proposal gate covers the MsgProp entry points of the Node API and nothing else. node.Propose, node.ProposeConfChange and node.Step with a MsgProp all end in stepWithWaitOption, which sends on n.propc, the channel the run loop only receives from while its gate variable is armed; every other message type, including MsgReadIndex, is sent on n.recvc instead. A member whose gate is disarmed therefore has its proposals and configuration proposals held, while its other duties - forwarding read-index requests to the leader it knows, answering messages and participating in replication and application - are not routed through the gate.",
        "conditions": "Async Node API run loop in this snapshot, whichever configuration flags are used, with the run loop's gate variable disarmed for the local member; the boundary is now backed by a retained read-index control on a re-added member (read state observed, proposal held), in addition to the source anchors.",
        "source_ids": [
          "stepwait",
          "propose_api",
          "runloop",
          "propc_uses",
          "removal_gate"
        ],
        "executions": {
